1296 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 619 0 R
/T (The survey includes a broad set of questions that enable analysis of how demographic characteristics, behaviors, health-related factors, and attitudes are associated with moderate- to high-risk gambling severity. These findings are largely consistent with those reported in FY2022-23.* Key findings are summarized below. )
/E (The survey includes a broad set of questions that enable analysis of how demographic characteristics, behaviors, health-related factors, and attitudes are associated with moderate- to high-risk gambling severity. These findings are largely consistent with those reported in FY2022-23.* Key findings are summarized below. )
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1297 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 619 0 R
/T (* This report includes several classes of variables that were not part of the FY2022-23 survey, including health and substance use factors that are strongly associated with problem gambling risk.)
/E (* This report includes several classes of variables that were not part of the FY2022-23 survey, including health and substance use factors that are strongly associated with problem gambling risk.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1298 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 619 0 R
/T (Several demographic characteristics were associated with elevated moderate- to high-risk gambling severity. Males were 1.5 times more likely than females to fall within the moderate- to high-risk categories \(22% versus 15%\). Risk also declined steadily with age, ranging from 26% among Arizonans aged 21-29 to 10% among those aged 60 and older.)
/E (Several demographic characteristics were associated with elevated moderate- to high-risk gambling severity. Males were 1.5 times more likely than females to fall within the moderate- to high-risk categories \(22% versus 15%\). Risk also declined steadily with age, ranging from 26% among Arizonans aged 21-29 to 10% among those aged 60 and older.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1299 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 619 0 R
/T (Socioeconomic factors were similarly associated with gambling risk. Arizonans living in households with incomes below $30,000 had the highest rate of moderate- to high-risk gambling severity \(29%\), compared to 16% among higher-income groups. Lower levels of educational attainment were also associated with greater risk, with rates ranging from 35% among those with less than a high school education to 15% among those with postgraduate education.)
/E (Socioeconomic factors were similarly associated with gambling risk. Arizonans living in households with incomes below $30,000 had the highest rate of moderate- to high-risk gambling severity \(29%\), compared to 16% among higher-income groups. Lower levels of educational attainment were also associated with greater risk, with rates ranging from 35% among those with less than a high school education to 15% among those with postgraduate education.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1300 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 619 0 R
/T (Differences were also observed across racial and ethnic groups. Arizonans identifying as Hispanic were nearly twice as likely to score in the moderate- to high-risk categories compared to non-Hispanic Whites \(27% versus 14%\). Non-Hispanic White respondents had the lowest statistically significant rate overall \(12%\), compared to 26% among all other racial and ethnic groups combined.)
/E (Differences were also observed across racial and ethnic groups. Arizonans identifying as Hispanic were nearly twice as likely to score in the moderate- to high-risk categories compared to non-Hispanic Whites \(27% versus 14%\). Non-Hispanic White respondents had the lowest statistically significant rate overall \(12%\), compared to 26% among all other racial and ethnic groups combined.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1301 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 619 0 R
/T (Marital status also showed meaningful variation. Arizonans who were separated \(36%\) or never married \(24%\) reported the highest rates of moderate- to high-risk gambling severity. Elevated risk among separated individuals may reflect the effects of financial strain, stress, or life disruption while the higher rates among never-married respondents may partially reflect the younger age profile of this group.)
/E (Marital status also showed meaningful variation. Arizonans who were separated \(36%\) or never married \(24%\) reported the highest rates of moderate- to high-risk gambling severity. Elevated risk among separated individuals may reflect the effects of financial strain, stress, or life disruption while the higher rates among never-married respondents may partially reflect the younger age profile of this group.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1302 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 619 0 R
/T (Demographic Characteristics)
/E (Demographic Characteristics)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1303 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 619 0 R
/T (Greater gambling frequency was strongly associated with higher PGSI risk levels for experiencing gambling-related harm. Rates of moderate- to high-risk PGSI classification ranged from 39% among individuals who gambled at least weekly to 7% among those who gambled once a month or less.)
/E (Greater gambling frequency was strongly associated with higher PGSI risk levels for experiencing gambling-related harm. Rates of moderate- to high-risk PGSI classification ranged from 39% among individuals who gambled at least weekly to 7% among those who gambled once a month or less.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
